# Engineering Quality Gates

These requirements are mandatory for every code-changing task.


## Validation Failures

If any validation fails:

* investigate the failure;
* fix failures caused by the current work;
* re-run the affected validation;
* do not claim the task is complete while any applicable validation remains red.

If a command cannot be run because of an environmental or tooling problem:

* clearly state that validation could not be completed;
* report the exact command that could not be run and the reason;
* do not describe the task as fully complete or verified.

Never claim that tests, coverage, linting, style linting, type checking, accessibility validation, Betterer, or a build passed unless the corresponding command was actually executed successfully during the current task.

## Lint and Type Safety

Never silence an error merely to obtain a green run.

Do not introduce any of the following as shortcuts:

* `eslint-disable`;
* `stylelint-disable`;
* `@ts-ignore`;
* `@ts-nocheck`;
* unnecessary `any`;
* disabled compiler checks;
* disabled lint rules;
* suppressed errors or warnings.

These mechanisms may only be used when there is a specific technical justification.

Fix the underlying problem whenever reasonably possible.

ESLint and Stylelint serve different purposes.

Passing ESLint does not substitute for passing Stylelint.

Passing Stylelint does not substitute for passing ESLint.

## Error Handling

Do not suppress errors merely to prevent failures.

Caught errors must be:

* handled meaningfully;
* transformed intentionally;
* or surfaced appropriately.

Never introduce empty catch blocks.

## Configuration Changes

Do not modify:

* ESLint configuration;
* Stylelint configuration;
* test runner configuration;
* TypeScript configuration;
* build tool configuration;
* Betterer configuration;
* `.betterer.results`;
* package scripts;
* compiler options;
* coverage configuration;
* quality-gate configuration;

merely to hide or bypass an error.

Configuration changes must solve an actual project requirement.

`.betterer.results` is an exception only when it is updated by normal `pnpm run betterer` execution because a tracked quality metric has genuinely improved.

Do not:

* manually edit `.betterer.results`;
* use Betterer update/force mechanisms to accept a regression;
* weaken Betterer tests;
* remove tracked metrics;
* add exclusions merely to make Betterer pass.

## Dependencies

Use pnpm for this repository.

Do not introduce a new runtime or development dependency unless it provides a clear benefit to the requested task.

Before adding a dependency:

* verify that the existing stack cannot reasonably solve the problem;
* prefer established, actively maintained packages;
* explain the reason for adding it.

Do not upgrade unrelated dependencies as part of another task.

## Automated Quality Gate

This repository has an automated stop hook that enforces the repository quality gates.

When the hook rejects completion:

* treat its output as authoritative;
* fix the reported failures before attempting completion again;
* do not attempt to bypass, disable, modify, or remove the hook;
* do not alter quality-gate scripts merely to circumvent a failure.

You have at most three automated repair attempts.

If all repair attempts are exhausted:

* stop modifying the code;
* do not claim the task is complete;
* provide the required failure debrief.

A failing quality gate must be resolved by fixing the underlying code, test, styling, types, configuration, build, accessibility issue, or other validated requirement.

Do not make a quality gate pass by:

* lowering coverage thresholds;
* disabling ESLint rules;
* disabling Stylelint rules;
* adding blanket lint suppressions;
* excluding files from coverage without justification;
* skipping tests;
* deleting tests;
* loosening assertions;
* changing TypeScript strictness;
* adding `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-nocheck`;
* disabling accessibility rules merely to make validation pass;
* modifying or bypassing the quality-gate hook.

## Quality Ratchet

This repository uses Betterer to prevent measurable code quality from regressing over time.

Betterer is a quality ratchet, not a replacement for the repository's absolute quality gates.

The current Betterer baseline stored in `.betterer.results` represents the minimum acceptable quality for all tracked metrics.

The Betterer quality gate is not green unless:

```bash
pnpm run betterer:ci
```

passes successfully after the test suite has produced its coverage results.

When Betterer reports a regression:

* fix the underlying regression;
* do not accept a worse baseline;
* do not modify the results file manually;
* do not use `betterer --update` or `betterer -u` to accept a regression;
* do not weaken or remove Betterer tests;
* do not exclude affected files merely to make the ratchet pass.

When quality has genuinely improved and `betterer:ci` reports that the committed baseline is stale:

* run `pnpm run betterer` without `--update`;
* allow Betterer to record the improved baseline;
* commit the resulting `.betterer.results` change;
* re-run `pnpm run betterer:ci`.

Normal Betterer execution may advance the baseline when quality improves.

It must never be used to accept a worse result.

Do not directly edit `.betterer.results`.

Do not modify `.betterer.ts`, Betterer scripts, tracked metrics, exclusions, or Betterer configuration as part of an unrelated task.

Changes to Betterer configuration are permitted only when they satisfy an explicit project requirement and do not weaken existing quality protections.

Passing the repository's fixed coverage threshold does not permit coverage to fall below the Betterer baseline.

Passing the repository quality gates requires both:

* all absolute quality gates to pass;
* all Betterer quality ratchets to remain equal to or better than their committed baselines.
